[{"id":"30071","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following was time period of Seventh five year plan of India?</span></p>","option_1":"1972-1977","option_2":"1975-1980","option_3":"1985-1990","option_4":"1995-2000","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1985-1990.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Seventh Five-Year Plan of India ran from 1985 to 1990. It focused on rapid growth in foodgrain production, employment generation, and productivity, with the slogan \"Food, Work and Productivity.\" It emphasized modernization of technology, self-reliance, and poverty reduction through direct intervention programs such as the Jawahar Rozgar Yojana, and achieved a growth rate of around 6%, higher than the targeted rate.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30072","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the meaning of the term 'Buddha'?</span></p>","option_1":"The powerful one","option_2":"The warrior monk","option_3":"The royal seeker","option_4":"The awakened one","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - The awakened one.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The word 'Buddha' derives from the Sanskrit/Pali root 'budh', meaning 'to awaken' or 'to know'. It refers to \"the awakened one\" or \"the enlightened one\" — a title given to Siddhartha Gautama after he attained enlightenment under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya. It is not a personal name but an epithet describing his awakened spiritual state.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30073","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The rivers that supported trade in Magadha were _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Ganga and Son","option_2":"Krishna and Kaveri","option_3":"Brahmaputra and Narmada","option_4":"Yamuna and Saraswati","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Ganga and Son.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Magadha, one of the sixteen Mahajanapadas, was strategically located between the Ganga and Son rivers, giving it access to fertile land, natural resources, and river-based trade routes. This riverine advantage, along with dense forests providing elephants and rich iron-ore deposits, was a key factor behind Magadha's rise as the most powerful of the Mahajanapadas.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30074","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What are the filtration units in kidneys which filters the waste in the human body?</span></p>","option_1":"Neuron","option_2":"Alveoli","option_3":"Nephrons","option_4":"Islets of Langerhans","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Nephrons.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Nephrons are the structural and functional units of the kidney responsible for filtering blood and forming urine. Each kidney contains about a million nephrons, each made up of a glomerulus (a cluster of capillaries) and a renal tubule. The glomerulus filters blood plasma, while the tubule reabsorbs useful substances and helps excrete waste as urine.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30075","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Generally, how many sessions of the House of the People of Indian Parliament take place in a year?</span></p>","option_1":"1","option_2":"3","option_3":"6","option_4":"8","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 3.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Though the Constitution does not fix an exact number, by convention Parliament (including the Lok Sabha) meets in three sessions each year: the Budget Session (February-May), the Monsoon Session (July-September), and the Winter Session (November-December). Article 85 only requires that the gap between two sessions must not exceed six months.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30076","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following historian divided Indian history into the Hindu, Muslim, and British periods?</span></p>","option_1":"James Rennel","option_2":"R.C. Majumdar","option_3":"V.D. Savarkar","option_4":"James Mill","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - James Mill.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>James Mill, a British utilitarian philosopher and historian, periodized Indian history into 'Hindu', 'Muslim', and 'British' civilizations in his work \"The History of British India\" (1817). This periodization has been widely criticized by later historians for being communal and Eurocentric, as it overlooked the syncretic, overlapping nature of Indian society.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30077","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The sad plays and songs popular in 19th-century Bihar's Bhojpur region are known as _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Chaiti","option_2":"Tappa","option_3":"Biraha","option_4":"Hori","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Biraha.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Biraha is a folk singing style originating in the Bhojpuri-speaking region of Bihar and eastern Uttar Pradesh, traditionally associated with themes of separation, longing, and sorrow (the word 'Biraha' itself means 'separation'). It was popularized further by artists like Bhikhari Thakur and remains an important part of Bhojpuri folk culture.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30078","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is an example of the effects of friction?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Worn-out shoes</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Worn-out steps of foot bridges at railway stations</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Friction causes wear and tear on surfaces that rub against each other repeatedly. Worn-out shoe soles result from friction between the shoe and the ground during walking, while worn-out steps of footbridges result from the constant friction of feet moving over the same surface. Both are classic real-life examples of the destructive effect of friction, which, despite being useful for grip and motion, also causes gradual erosion of material.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30079","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Among the scheduled languages the smallest language groups in term of speakers are _____</span></p>","option_1":"Sanskrit, Bodo, and Manipuri","option_2":"Telugu, Marathi, and Gujarati","option_3":"Urdu, Assamese, and Malayalam","option_4":"Konkani, Maithili, and Sindhi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Sanskrit, Bodo, and Manipuri.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Census of India data on scheduled languages, among the 22 languages listed in the Eighth Schedule, Sanskrit, Bodo, and Manipuri (Meitei) have comparatively very small numbers of speakers relative to major languages like Hindi, Bengali, or Telugu. Sanskrit in particular has an extremely small number of native speakers as recorded in the census.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30080","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Poona Pact is related to which of the following?</span></p>","option_1":"Mahatma Gandhi and Motilal Nehru","option_2":"Mahatma Gandhi and Shaukat Ali","option_3":"Motilal Nehru and C.R. Das","option_4":"Mahatma Gandhi and B.R. Ambedkar","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Mahatma Gandhi and B.R. Ambedkar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Poona Pact (1932) was an agreement between Mahatma Gandhi and Dr. B.R. Ambedkar, signed after Gandhi undertook a fast unto death against the Communal Award, which had granted separate electorates to the \"Depressed Classes\" (Dalits). The pact replaced separate electorates with reserved seats for depressed classes within the general electorate, increasing their representation in provincial legislatures.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30081","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>With respect to languages in India, what does \"SPPEL\" stand for?</span></p>","option_1":"Scheme for Protection and Preservation of Endangered Languages","option_2":"Special Program for Promotion of Eastern Languages","option_3":"Scheme for Popularization and Preservation of Emerging Literature","option_4":"State Policy for Promotion of Endangered Literature","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Scheme for Protection and Preservation of Endangered Languages.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>SPPEL (Scheme for Protection and Preservation of Endangered Languages) is a scheme launched by the Ministry of Education, implemented through the Central Institute of Indian Languages (CIIL), Mysuru, to document and preserve languages spoken by fewer than 10,000 people that are at risk of extinction across India.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30082","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The rate of motion of an object is to find out the distance travelled by the object in unit time. What is this quantity known as?</span></p>","option_1":"Distance","option_2":"Speed","option_3":"Displacement","option_4":"Velocity","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Speed.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Speed is defined as the distance travelled by an object per unit time; it is a scalar quantity having only magnitude. This distinguishes it from velocity, which is a vector quantity referring to the rate of change of displacement, having both magnitude and direction.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30083","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"788","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is 'GPS spoofing', that was reported near India's borders with Pakistan and Myanmar in late 2024?</span></p>","option_1":"A method to improve GPS accuracy","option_2":"A procedure to block internet signals","option_3":"A satellite repair technique","option_4":"A cyber-attack involving false GPS signals to mislead navigation equipment","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - A cyber-attack involving false GPS signals to mislead navigation equipment.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>GPS spoofing is a technique where fake GPS signals are transmitted to deceive a GPS receiver into calculating an incorrect position, time, or velocity. Such incidents were reported near India's borders with Pakistan and Myanmar in late 2024, raising security concerns, as spoofing can disrupt navigation for aircraft, ships, and other systems relying on GPS, posing risks to both civilian and defence operations.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"",